Sval: downloads decode Content-Encoding whatever the file's extension

Backport of Mozilla's Bug 1470011 (mozilla-central bca87fa82d9e, Firefox
154), unchanged. ESR 153 does not carry it.
ApplyDecodingForExtension kept a gzip Content-Encoding on the saved file
when the URL's extension matched it (.gz, .tgz, .zip, .z, .svgz), so a
server that gzipped a .tar.gz again in transit left a doubly compressed
file. The new pref
network.http.decode_content_for_known_compressed_extensions, true by
default, decodes it as Chrome does; false keeps the old behaviour. Its
tests are in common_test_Download.js, which test_DownloadCore.js and
test_DownloadLegacy.js run.
Drop this patch when the upstream pin reaches Firefox 154.

diff --git a/modules/libpref/init/StaticPrefList.yaml b/modules/libpref/init/StaticPrefList.yaml
index 8c6f50aad8..6631a1b163 100644
--- a/modules/libpref/init/StaticPrefList.yaml
+++ b/modules/libpref/init/StaticPrefList.yaml
@@ -14715,6 +14715,15 @@
   value: 393216
   mirror: always
 
+# When true (default), decode Content-Encoding (e.g. gzip) when saving to disk
+# even if the URL extension matches the encoding (.gz, .tgz, .zip, .z, .svgz),
+# avoiding double-compressed files. When false, restore the legacy behavior of
+# not decoding. See bug 610679, bug 1470011.
+- name: network.http.decode_content_for_known_compressed_extensions
+  type: RelaxedAtomicBool
+  value: true
+  mirror: always
+
 # Whether or not we give more priority to active tab.
 - name: network.http.active_tab_priority
   type: RelaxedAtomicBool
